From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sat, 27 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] generate_qmap: add the phone toolbelt keys

Qt's evdev keyboard handler uses the keymap it is given and nothing else: a
keycode with no entry is dropped before any Qt key event exists. The keymap
this tool writes is Qt's default table plus webos_keymap[] below, and between
them they cover no call, back, home or app-select key at all.

So on a phone with a hardware toolbelt none of those keys do anything, however
the shell is written, because the shell never sees them. Measured on the Zinwa
Q25, a BlackBerry Classic restomod, whose toolbelt reaches evdev perfectly
well - the driver reports every key:

    Answer (green)    scancode 0x06 -> keycode 231  KEY_SEND
    BlackBerry logo   scancode 0x11 -> keycode 580  KEY_APPSELECT
    Back              scancode 0x07 -> keycode 158  KEY_BACK
    Hangup (red)      scancode 0x12 -> keycode 172  KEY_HOMEPAGE

and are then discarded here. On that phone KEY_MUTE is the one hardware key of
the five that works, and the only one already in the map - through Qt's default
table rather than this one.

The Qt codes below are the honest reading of each *keycode*, not of what the
key is labelled on any one device, and that distinction matters: the Q25's
hangup key emits KEY_HOMEPAGE and its menu key emits KEY_APPSELECT, which is
its keyboard controller's doing. Binding 172 to Key_Hangup would therefore be
right for that phone and wrong for every other one. Each keycode gets a
distinct Qt key here, and what it means stays a per-device decision above this
layer.

Key_Menu for KEY_APPSELECT is the nearest thing Qt offers, having no
app-switcher key. KEY_MENU (139) is in neither table, so nothing collides
today; anything adding it later should give one of the two a different Qt code
rather than leaving them indistinguishable.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 tools/generate_qmap/webos_keymap_p.h | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/tools/generate_qmap/webos_keymap_p.h b/tools/generate_qmap/webos_keymap_p.h
--- a/tools/generate_qmap/webos_keymap_p.h
+++ b/tools/generate_qmap/webos_keymap_p.h
@@ -21,7 +21,16 @@
 // { keycode, unicode, qtcode, modifiers, flags, special }
 const QEvdevKeyboardMap::Mapping webos_keymap[] = {
     { 122, 0xffff, Qt::Key_Hangul,  0x00, 0x00, 0x0000 }, // Hangul
-    { 125, 0xffff, Qt::Key_Super_L, 0x00, 0x00, 0x0000 }  // LEFT_META (Windows key)
+    { 125, 0xffff, Qt::Key_Super_L, 0x00, 0x00, 0x0000 }, // LEFT_META (Windows key)
+
+    // The hardware toolbelt of a phone with physical call keys. Qt's default
+    // table has none of these, so without them the keys never become Qt key
+    // events at all. What each one is used for is decided per device, above
+    // this layer: these are the keycodes' own meanings, not one phone's labels.
+    { 158, 0xffff, Qt::Key_Back,     0x00, 0x00, 0x0000 }, // KEY_BACK
+    { 172, 0xffff, Qt::Key_HomePage, 0x00, 0x00, 0x0000 }, // KEY_HOMEPAGE
+    { 231, 0xffff, Qt::Key_Call,     0x00, 0x00, 0x0000 }, // KEY_SEND
+    { 580, 0xffff, Qt::Key_Menu,     0x00, 0x00, 0x0000 }  // KEY_APPSELECT
 };
 
 #endif // WEBOS_KEYMAP_P_H
